  "unit": "u06-bazaar",
  "title": "Clothes shopping",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "The future for 'I' ends in **-ব** (*-bo*): **কিনব** (*kinbo*) 'I will buy'. For আপনি it's **-বেন**: **কিনবেন** 'you will buy'. Put **না** after the verb to negate: **কিনব না** 'I won't buy'. (You'll also see the spelling কিনবো.)\n\nUseful words: **শাড়ি** (sari), **জামা** (shirt / top / dress), **লুঙ্গি** (the wrap many Bangladeshi men wear at home). Add **-টা** for 'the one': **শাড়িটা** 'the sari', **জামাটা** 'the shirt'. Colours go before the noun: **লাল শাড়ি** 'a red sari'. Careful: **শাড়ি** (*shari*) sounds close to **সাড়ে** (*share*, 'half past').\n\nBefore paying with a big note, ask **ভাংতি আছে?** 'Do you have change?' **ভাংতি** and **খুচরা** both mean 'change' (small notes and coins), you'll hear both, and both are right. In Kolkata it's **খুচরো** (*khuchro*).\n\n**কোনো … নেই** means 'no … at all': **কোনো সমস্যা নেই** 'no problem at all'.\n\n**Culture:** clothing markets are where bargaining is most expected, so it's normal to ask the price of several saris before you choose. Red-and-white saris are the classic choice for Pohela Boishakh (Bengali New Year), which you'll meet in Unit 10.",
